-in  scope now this scope contains names such  as keywords functions exceptions and  other attributes that are built into  python  now if you define the same variable name  inside and outside of a function python  will treat them as separate variables so  two separate variables one available in  the global scope that is outside the  function and the other available in the  local scope that is inside the function  now let's head over to our jupiter  notebook and we'll try  scopes in python with hands-on  demonstrations  all right so i'm on my jupyter notebook  let's start with our  examples first we'll look at  local scope in python  for that i am going to create a function  called  fun  and  inside the function i will create a  variable called  s which  holds let's say  an object called  simply loan  and then  i am going to  print  the variable that is s  now in this case i have defined my  variable  inside the function so this becomes  accessible only  inside the function  now if i call the function using the  function name that is f u n  and if we run it you can see we have the  message printed that is simply known  now  we'll see another example  so suppose  we'll try to access it outside the  function it will throw an error so i'll  just give a comment try  to access  it  outside the function  all right  so again i'll create  a function called let's say  fun 1  i'll give a colon and here i'll say  let's say  s1 equal to simply loan  and now  i'll give a comment here print s1  then i'll call my function  f u n 1  and  i'll try to  print  the variable s1  outside the function so here s1  has a local scope since it is defined  inside the function but i am trying to  access the variable outside the function  let's run it  you can see the error here name s1 not  defined  now  if i give a  comment  and run this  you see there is no message and let me  just remove this comment and print it  you can see we have the  s1 variable message printed that is  simply known so this is how the  local scope of a function works  now let's see an example of  enclosed scope which is  function inside another function  so i'll create a function  let's say func  and  i'll use the variable this time s2 equal  to  simply loan  and then  inside the func function i will define  another function let's say  i n func which basically stands for  inside  function and then here i'll say print s  s2  and  we'll call the  inner function that is in  fu and c  and then i'll call the outer function  let's run it  and see the result  you can see here we have the message  printed  simply learn i just  edit this message let's say  welcome to simply loan  now if i run this  you can see we have updated the message  here  now  for the enclosed scope you need to  define an outer function including the  inner function like we  did here  and you can also comment out the local  variable of the inner function and refer  to the variable using the non-local  keyword let me show you an example  around this  suppose i have a  global variable called msg  i'll see this is a  global  scope  message  after that  i'll create a function so i'll define a  function called  outer  and  i'll use the same variable name that is  msg  and my message would be this is an  outer variable  after that  i'm going to define another function  called  inner  and  this will have  the same variable that is msg and my  message would be  inner  message  variable  now as i mentioned before i need to  comment out the  local variable of the inner function so  i'll just  comment this out  and then  i'm going to say  non-local which is the keyboard  so i am  referring to the variable using the  non-local keyword  i'll say non-local  variable name that is msg  and now  i'll  print msg  will come out of the function and we'll  call it  inner  after that we'll call the  outer function  and then i'm going to print i'll just  scroll this down  and we'll say msg  so here what we are doing is so this is  an example of an enclosed scope so where  we have  defined the outer function enclosing the  inner function and we have commented out  the local variable of the inner function  and we are referring to the variable  using the non-local keyboard now let's  run it you see here i have  my  two  print statements working here the first  one is this is an outer variable you can  see here and then i have print msg so  this is a global scope message cool  now  we'll see an example of how the global  scope works though we have already seen  an example above so this was an example  of a global  scope  now we'll see a few more examples around  global scope again  suppose i have a variable called s3  equal to i'll see  simply loan  and then i'll declare a function  f unc  one  i give a colon and then say  print  s three  now i'll call the function f u n c one  and will print it  s3  if i run it you can see here i have  simply learned printed twice so i have  used the same variable name s3  inside the function so  this simply learn is for the  local scope s3 that is this one and  outside the  function we have printed s3 again so  simply learn twice  all right  now let me show you another example  about using the  global scope  create a variable msg1  let's say my message is  welcome to  simply learn  python  programming  i'll define the function func 2 i'll  give a colon  and i'll use the same variable that i  used  or declared above that is msg1  i'll say this is the  inner msg1 variable  then i'm going to print  msg1  after that i'll call my function func 2  and i'll print  msg1  if i run this  you can see i have the two messages  printed first is this is the inner mst1  variable and the second print statement  will print the  global variable  message that is welcome to simply learn  python programming  cool  now you can also  use the global keyword inside the  function to convert a local scope into  global scope  now  i'll just give a comment using  global keyword  so i'll define my function func let's  say 3  i'll give a colon  and using the global keyword  i'll say  s  and i'll say s equal to  simply loan  now let me  call func 3  and will print s outside the function  now one thing to remember here  my variable has been declared inside the  function but since before declaring the  variable i have assigned the global  keyword to s  so this now becomes a  global scope variable so you can access  it outside the function as well now if i  run it you can see it here i have my  message printed  simply on if i say hello you can see it  hello printed  cool  now  we'll see another example of using the  global scope  let's say i have a variable called a1  which is  india  and then  i'll create a function called my country  and i'll use  the  global keyword and say a1  and after that  i'm going to  say a1 equal to  i'll change the country to let's say  france  now let me  call the function my country  and will print  a1  and you can  notice the difference in the result so  here initially i had defined india as a  global variable and after that i am  assigning the  same variable name with a global keyword  and hence it will take  the updated value for e1 and you can see  here the result is france  now finally let's look at  built-in scope in python  this is the final example in the demo  session about scopes and arguments in  python  cool  so  this example is a bit different from the  previous few examples that we saw on  local scope  enclosed scope and global scope  so here  i am going to  import  pi from the math  module so i will say from math  import  pi  so this example includes  global scope enclosed scope as well as  local scope and of course we have the  built-in scope as well  now what i'll do is first i'll  give a comment and  create a variable called  pi  equal to  i'll say  global  pi  variable  i'll say this is a global  pi variable  and then  what i'll do is i'll say  df  let's do one thing first of all we'll  remove  the comment  and now i'll create a  outer function  into a colon and say  the variable pi  i'll say  outer  variable  pi  and then  i'll create  a nested function with the name inner  and  i'll say pi  equal to  inner  by variable  now  i am going to say  print  pi  and  after that i'll  call the  inner function  and  next  i'll also call the  outer function  now if i  run this  you can see the result  inner  pi variable being printed which is this  print statement  now  what i'll do is  i'm going to comment out the  global scope variable  and i'll also comment out  the first  outer function variable that i defined  i'll comment this out  and i'll also comment the inner  function  pi variable  now  if i run this  you see the result here it prints the  value of pi that was imported directly  from the math module or the math library  so here pi is not defined in either  local  enclosed or  global scope so the built-in scope is  looked up that is the pi value imported  from the math module so this is an  example of built-in scope in python that  uses  inbuilt  functions modules and objects that are  available in python  all right  so that brings us to the end of this  video tutorial on python scopes and  arguments i hope this video was useful  and informative if you have any queries  please feel free to put them in the  comments section of the video thank you  for watching  hi there if you like this video  subscribe to the simply learn youtube  channel and click here to watch similar  videos to nerd up and get certified  click here

